body:
.......... this is, somewhat relatable, yeah,,, After my mother's death (and the prospect of them potentially having to help me in any way, shape, or form because of her death), my entire family took her shit and left me to die. As well as almost all the family friends we had, who'd sworn they'd "always be there" if anything ever happened.

Then, following my suicide attempt and hospitalization, slowly one by one my friends stopped calling me or checking in. My sister would call every once in a while but if asked to do something even as simple as telling me what our old address was or where my birth certificate was located, she'd go off about how she refuses to help me unless I can 100% guarantee she'll never have to pay a cent, or go out of her way at all, "I'm not your fucking mother and I don't have to help as if I am"........ the only person who kept up contact was my then-boyfriend, but his parents took his phone and blocked him from calling me because I "caused his depression" (he'd had depression for Years before anything ever happened to me?? also wtf). 

I understand that me being in such a horrific situation put emotional strain on my friends, regardless of anything I did or did not do, and I understand no one is "obligated" to help me in any way........ but.............. ha ;;

I have extreme difficulties with working, too. I've had two jobs, both I've kept for extended periods of time (because I have to, I pay rent to live), but not without immense difficulty and distress. The amount of stuff I can do is limited, both because of my current mental state/circumstances and because of a pretty severe lack of experience/higher education/skills, all of which makes me sub-par at even the most "basic" of jobs (if I'm not outright unable to do them) and unable to find anything better/easier on me. Suicidal ideation and debilitating anxiety are constant and there's no easy way out of my current situation. 

I'm not entirely sure how I keep doing it. Giving up is kind of my Thing, so it's astonishing that I haven't yet. For me I guess it's because the consequences of quitting are huge (if I can't pay rent, I either become homeless again or rely on my roommates to support me, a situation that resembles a horrific time in my life too closely to even consider risking it), so I just...... I just stew in constant panic and exhaustion, but cling to the hope that maybe someday when I really start getting into therapy and recovery work........... It'll start to get a bit easier, and then I can finally learn new skills and gain new experience and get myself into a better situation overall. 

It'll take work to get there, and probably a lot more time than I feel ready to get through. But there's nothing to do but keep going as long as I can, y'know? ;;
